<Layout title="Pattern Match Learner" description="A small neural network learns to classify 8×8 pixel patterns with gradient descent, shown step by step in the browser." headingTracker={false}>
        <div class="pattern-wrapper">
        <div class="pml-container">
            <div class="explanation">
                <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px dotted #000; padding-bottom: 8px;">
                    <h1 style="margin: 0; border: none; padding: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--fg-strong);">Pattern Match Learner</h1>
                    <a href="/lab/ai/" style="font-size: 14px;">← AI lab</a>
                </div>

                <p>A small neural network learns to sort 8×8 black-and-white patterns into six types, in your browser.</p>

                <ul style="margin: 0 0 16px; padding-left: 20px; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--fg);">
                    <li><strong>Data:</strong> 300 generated patterns, 50 of each type. Each has random noise: some pixels flipped on or off.</li>
                    <li><strong>Network:</strong> 64 inputs (one per pixel), one hidden layer with ReLU, 6 outputs with softmax.</li>
                    <li><strong>Training:</strong> stochastic gradient descent on cross-entropy loss, batches of 4 random patterns.</li>
                    <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> measured on the same 300 patterns it trains on. There is no separate test set.</li>
                </ul>

                <p>
                    Press <strong>Start learning</strong> and watch the loss fall and the weights change.
                    Press <strong>Reset network</strong> to start again from new random weights. The network
                    usually reaches similar accuracy with different weights each time.
                </p>
            </div>

